S=[
("Madison County in brief","""<p>Up in north central Alabama, Madison County counts 433,516 people in the 2025 estimate, a total beaten only by Jefferson County. Huntsville serves as the seat and is also the biggest city. Redstone Arsenal and the defense and space contractors around it have supplied tens of thousands of jobs, and that steady employment makes house sales here behave differently from those in a typical rural Alabama county.</p><p>Housing totals 169,820 units, with 7.1% sitting vacant. Among occupied homes, 66.3% have owners and 33.7% have renters. Since a third of occupied homes are rentals, a tenant is often living in a house when it sells, and that shapes the closing schedule.</p><p>The cities are Huntsville, Madison, New Hope and Owens Cross Roads, with Gurley and Triana as towns. Harvest, Hazel Green, Meridianville, Moores Mill and New Market are communities without a municipal government. Whether a city levy lands on your bill depends on the address on your deed. School service is split three ways: Madison County Schools handle the unincorporated areas along with Gurley, New Hope and Owens Cross Roads, Huntsville City Schools cover most of Huntsville, and Madison City Schools also serve Triana.</p>"""),
("Due dates and penalties","""<p>The due date is October 1 and the delinquency line is December 31. Several details tend to catch new owners off guard.</p><ul><li>Mailing a bill before delinquency is not a legal requirement. A courtesy notice goes out by October 1, yet if it never reaches you the tax is still late.</li><li>Whoever owned the parcel on October 1 of the prior year is the name on the bill, so a past owner may keep getting it.</li><li>Each bill spans the stretch from last October 1 to September 30 of the present year.</li><li>Once December 31 is behind you, expect a $5.00 late fee, monthly interest of 1% on the base amount, and probate and advertising fees.</li></ul><p>After delinquency the account follows the state calendar: probate court in February, advertising in April and sale in May. Lien sales happen only online. Anyone listing a house in winter should check for an unpaid year beforehand, since the payoff climbs every month after January 1.</p>"""),
("Tax rates by location","""<p>The posted rates are per $100 of assessed value, and a Huntsville address and a rural one are far apart. Resets happen, so ask the collector what is current.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Rate per $100 assessed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Huntsville</td><td>$5.80</td></tr><tr><td>Madison</td><td>$6.95</td></tr><tr><td>Triana</td><td>$6.15</td></tr><tr><td>Gurley, New Hope, Owens Cross Roads</td><td>$4.05</td></tr><tr><td>Unincorporated, land</td><td>$3.35</td></tr><tr><td>Unincorporated, improvements</td><td>$3.65</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The order of steps is: classification percentage times appraised value gives assessed value, then the rate is applied, and exemptions come off. At equal prices, a house in the city of Madison and one in Huntsville produce noticeably different yearly bills.</p>"""),
("How to appeal your assessment","""<p>Put your appeal in writing and take it to the Board of Equalization, on the 2nd floor of the Service Center at 1918 Memorial Parkway NW, Huntsville. Code of Alabama sections 40-3-20, 40-3-24 and 40-3-25 govern the process.</p><p>Values are reset annually, normally in March or April, and a legal ad runs in the newspaper two weeks in a row. Your 30 days to file begin with the second ad. An appraiser then reaches out to go over the value, and a board hearing follows if the disagreement remains.</p><p>If the board rules against you, circuit court is next. Section 40-3-25 gives 30 days from the final decision, and you notify the board secretary and the circuit clerk. Request a jury trial within 10 days of appealing. After the circuit judgment you have 42 days to go on to the Alabama Supreme Court.</p><p>One rule trips people up. To keep the circuit appeal alive, pay the taxes by December 31 or post a bond for double the tax due. If you sell mid-appeal, settle that question with your closing attorney first.</p>"""),
("Homestead exemptions","""<p>The state allows a homestead exemption for a single-family house that is your own primary residence on the tax year's first day, on as many as 160 acres. Applications go to the county office rather than the state.</p><ul><li>The standard exemption for owners under 65 removes up to $4,000 of assessed value from the state portion and, typically, $2,000 from the county portion.</li><li>Larger relief is possible at 65 and older, depending on income, under types H-2, H-3 and H-4.</li><li>A permanent and total disability qualifies an owner at any age, and blind owners get state-level relief.</li></ul><p>With the regular exemption, a bill with assessed value of $4,000 or more is reduced by $48 in state and county taxes. After buying, make sure of three things: the deed is on file at the probate office, the Tax Assessor's office has assessed the property, and your exemption has been claimed. A closing attorney often does this, but the responsibility rests with the owner.</p>"""),
("Where and how to pay","""<p>Payments can be made online, by mail, or at the counter in the Service Center or the satellite office at 100 Plaza Blvd. in Madison. Hours at both are 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., Monday to Friday. The main line is 256-532-3370.</p><ul><li>Card payments cost a 2.75% convenience fee plus $0.30 each, and the county keeps none of it.</li><li>An e-check costs $1.50.</li><li>The office no longer takes money orders.</li><li>For real property in April and May, only certified funds, cash, debit or credit cards work; personal checks are refused.</li><li>A mailed payment needs a December 31 postmark to dodge the late fee and interest.</li></ul><p>Checks are payable to the Madison County Tax Collector. If you hold no bill, email mctaxcoll@madisoncountyal.gov or phone, because the amount due after December 31 moves with fees and interest.</p>"""),
("Selling with taxes owed","""<p>Closing agents locate any unpaid tax and deduct it from what you receive. Because Alabama bills in arrears, the tax that falls due next October 1 is already building up while the house is yours.</p><p>Common snags on Madison County sales include these.</p><ul><li>An earlier year is unpaid and now carries interest, a late fee and advertising costs.</li><li>An inherited house whose tax bill still names a deceased parent.</li><li>A tenant occupies the home and the closing has to fit the lease.</li><li>The parcel sits on the delinquent list ahead of the May lien sale.</li></ul><p>A cash buyer can work around each of these and settle with the county out of the proceeds, so nothing comes from your pocket at the table. Begin by calling the collector for an exact payoff.</p><p>Cash Home Buyers writes offers on Madison County houses as they are. A lender-free sale means no appraisal wait and no repair list.</p>"""),
]
F=[("What is the due date for Madison County property taxes?","October 1 is the date. After December 31 the bill is delinquent and picks up a $5.00 late fee and 1% monthly interest on the base amount."),
("Where do I file a property value appeal in Madison County?","Go to the Board of Equalization in the Service Center, 1918 Memorial Parkway NW, Huntsville. Thirty days run from the second newspaper advertisement of new values."),
("Does Madison County mail a tax bill?","Only a courtesy notice is sent, by October 1, and no bill is required before delinquency. Look the parcel up online or call 256-532-3370 if nothing came."),
("What happens if I do not pay Madison County property tax?","Interest and fees build up, and parcels still unpaid may be offered at the online tax lien sale each May."),
("Can I sell my Madison County house if taxes are overdue?","Yes. Closing proceeds cover the overdue amount. Get the exact payoff from the collector before picking an asking price.")]
w('madison-county-al-property-tax-guide',S,F)
